{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when the Ark set forward</b> – He made for it a sign before it and after it, to indicate that this is not its place. And why was it written here? In order to separate between punishment and punishment, as is stated in the case of all sacred writings (Shabbat 116a) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 10:35). But the Rav did not explain to us what this punishment was that required separation, for no punishment is mentioned in the text before \"And it came to pass, when the Ark set forward.\" And the language of the Gemara there is: the second punishment is \"And the people were as complainers\" (Numbers 11:1); the first punishment is as it is written, \"And they set forward from the mount of YHWH\" (Numbers 10:33), and Rabbi Chanina said: this teaches that they turned away from following YHWH. And the Rav wrote in his commentary there: within three days of their journey, the mixed multitude craved a craving, to complain about the meat in order to rebel against YHWH. And these words are astonishing, for the punishment of \"And the people were as complainers\" is written first, and that of the craving second, and the two are adjacent. Perhaps the Rav held that they were written out of order — he alluded to the first one when he said \"from the mount of YHWH,\" for perhaps from the time of their journeying they planned to do so, and he interrupted and wrote the second one, and afterward returned to the first. But there is no sense or substance in this. Rather, the substance of this midrash they found in the Aggadah: they journeyed from Mount Sinai with joy like a child running away from school. They said: perhaps He will increase and give us more commandments. And this is \"And they set forward from the mount of YHWH\" — that their intention in journeying from there was because it is the mount of YHWH. And this is the first punishment, and He made a separation so that there would not be three punishments adjacent to one another, and they would be found entrenched in punishment. And He called the sin \"punishment\" even though no punishment befell them on account of it, and perhaps had it not been for this sin of theirs, it would have brought them into the Land immediately."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:13.348975690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}